# Nature Methods discipline

Parent paper target is a Nature Methods *Article*. Apply these limits when a chapter will migrate into that paper, unless the user overrides them. LaTeX later uses Springer Nature `sn-jnl` / `sn-nature`.

| Element | Limit |
|---------|--------|
| Abstract | ≤ 150 words, unreferenced |
| Main text | ~3,000 words (up to ~5,000); excludes abstract, Methods, references, legends |
| Footnotes | None in the main article |
| References | ≤ 50 |
| Display items | ≤ 6 figures and/or tables combined |

Fold background into the Introduction. Skeletons: Abstract IMRaD; Introduction context → aim → gap → direction; Methods population → approach → steps → tools → evaluation; Results and discussion summary → interpretation → prior work → limitations; close by restating the problem, insights, and significance, with no new results.

Validation is comparative (baselines, ablation, or controls) with seeds, versions, and data availability. Cite every display item. Figure captions must stand alone (axes, labels, colours, panels). Prefer vector PDF/SVG. Bar charts include 0. Mute gridlines; no dual-y axes; greyscale- and CVD-safe colours; no red–green encoding. One question per panel.

When asked to critique: 1–4 major and 2–6 minor issues, covering claims, baselines, methods, validation, venue fit, and a one-sentence recommendation.

Simulations: link the DeCovarT vignette `how-to-build-synthetic-scenarios-mean-covariance.qmd` `{#sec-nmeth}` (DGM, estimand, L'Ecuyer-CMRG, Monte Carlo error). Do not duplicate that table. rOpenSci `@srrstats` tags live in the package. Reproducibility here means `renv.lock`, `DeCovarT::fit_decovart()` / `deconvolute_ratios()`, evaluation scripts, and small fixtures under `data/processed/` when needed.
